<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # The Book Is on the Table. – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Discovering Our Classroom</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> The Book Is on the Table.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Follow oral directions for simple games. Interactive: Present responses based on listened questions.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can follow game directions to place or find objects accurately (e.g., "Put the pencil in the bag."). Can create and share responses about object locations (e.g., "What's on your desk?" "A notebook."). Can listen and respond to questions about object locations (e.g., "Where is the book?" "On the table.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 25px; margin-bottom: 15px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 35%; text-align: left;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; text-align: left;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 15%; text-align: left;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Listen and Touch" activity. The teacher says, "Touch your desk!", "Touch a chair!", "Touch the board!". Students listen and touch the specified classroom objects. This activates prior knowledge of classroom items and focuses on listening for key nouns.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a book and a desk. The teacher places the book on the desk and says clearly, "The book is on the desk." The teacher points to the book, then the desk, and repeats. The teacher then places a pencil in a pencil case and says, "The pencil is in the pencil case." The teacher deliberately emphasizes the pronunciation of /b/ in "book" and /t/ in "table" (using 'desk' as a parallel for 'table') and the preposition "on" /ɔn/ and "in". The teacher uses gestures to show "on" and "in".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the new vocabulary and prepositions.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">For "on": The teacher places a book under the desk and asks, "Is the book on the desk?" (No). Then places it correctly and asks, "Is the book on the desk?" (Yes).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">For "book": The teacher holds up an eraser and asks, "Is this a book?" (No). Then holds up a book and asks, "What is this?" (A book).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">For "in": The teacher holds up an empty pencil case and asks, "Is the pencil in the pencil case?" (No). Then puts a pencil inside and asks, "Where is the pencil?" (In the pencil case).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short audio recording (or teacher-read script) of a dialogue between two 3rd-grade students, Ana and Tom, discovering items and their locations in their new classroom. The dialogue includes: "Look, a desk! And a chair! What's on the desk? A book! The book is on the desk. Where is my pencil? Oh, it's in the pencil case. And a marker! The marker is on the board. Wow, a map! The map is on the wall, next to the board. My notebook is on my chair." The teacher ensures clear pronunciation of /b/, /t/, and prepositions (on, in, next to).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Locating objects and places: Using appropriate prepositions to describe familiar object places (e.g., "The book is on the table."). Confirming object locations in the classroom: "It's here." "It's on the shelf."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist:</b> After the first listening, the teacher asks, "What are Ana and Tom talking about?" (Classroom objects). "Where are they?" (In the classroom). Students can point to pictures or give one-word answers.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> The teacher plays the audio again. The teacher says statements, and students listen to the audio to determine if the statement is true or false. Students show "True" (thumbs up) or "False" (thumbs down) cards.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The book is on the chair." (False)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The pencil is in the pencil case." (True)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The marker is on the board." (True)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listen and Point:</b> The teacher plays parts of the audio or repeats sentences. Students listen and point to the corresponding objects and locations in the classroom or on a large classroom poster. E.g., Teacher: "The book is on the desk." Students point to a book on a desk.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Listening)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> WH-questions (What's in your bag?, Where is the book?), Prepositions of place (on, in, next to, behind), Present simple (The book is...). Vocabulary: desk, book, marker, board, shelf, eraser, paper, chair, crayon, notebook, ruler, pencil, pencil case, pen, map. Pronunciation focus on /b/, /t/, and blending of phonemes in 'on' /ɔn/ and 'paper' /ˈp-eɪp-ər/.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice – "Teacher Says, Students Do":</b> The teacher gives oral instructions using classroom objects and prepositions. Students listen and perform the action using real objects. (e.g., "Put the book on the chair.", "Place the pencil in the pencil case.", "Hold the ruler next to the notebook."). The teacher ensures students accurately follow directions, focusing on receptive listening to differentiate between prepositions and objects.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice – "Listen and Choose":</b> The teacher displays a few classroom objects and a few locations (e.g., two books, one on a chair, one under a desk; a pencil case with a pen inside, another empty). The teacher says, "Listen: The book is on the chair." Students listen and point to the correct picture or object arrangement. The teacher also asks, "What's in the pencil case?" and students listen and respond with "A pen."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction – "Where is it?":</b> Students work in pairs with a small set of classroom objects (e.g., a pencil, an eraser, a small notebook, a pencil case). One student (A) secretly places an object (e.g., the eraser) in/on/next to another object (e.g., in the pencil case). Student B listens and asks, "Where is the eraser?" Student A listens to the question and gives a short, simple answer: "It's in the pencil case." Then they switch roles. The teacher circulates, modeling "Excuse me, where is...?" if needed for polite interaction.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: "Classroom Scavenger Hunt" (Listening Focus):</b> The teacher orally gives a series of simple directions to find "hidden" classroom objects. Students listen carefully and follow the directions.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher divides students into small groups (3-4 students). Each group receives a small picture card of a classroom object (e.g., a marker, a ruler, a crayon).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher gives oral directions for where to *find* a specific object. For example: "Listen! Find the book! It's on the shelf, next to the map." or "Listen! Find the pencil! It's in the pencil case, on the small desk."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students in each group listen intently to the full instruction, discuss briefly if needed (in English, e.g., "Shelf? Map?"), and then move to locate the object.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Once a group finds their object, they bring it back to the teacher. The teacher asks, "Where was the book?" and the group responds, "It was on the shelf!" or "On the shelf."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">This activity directly connects to the theme "The Book Is on the Table" and scenario "Discovering Our Classroom," requiring students to listen to locate objects and confirm their findings verbally.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy and student reflection)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy: Observation Checklist.</b> The teacher uses a simple checklist to note students' ability to follow oral directions and provide simple responses during the "Classroom Scavenger Hunt" and a final "Listen and Draw" activity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task: "Listen and Draw/Place."</b> The teacher gives three to four oral instructions. Students listen and either draw the object in the correct location on a simple classroom outline worksheet or place actual classroom items as instructed.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Draw a book on the desk."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Draw a pencil in the pencil case."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Draw a marker on the board."</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate following of oral instructions (e.g., drawing/placing objects in the correct prepositional location).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of key vocabulary (book, table, on, in) in short responses (if applicable).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of identifying objects and locations based on listening input.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration and respectful listening within group activities (Sociolinguistic competence).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a simple exit ticket by circling a happy, neutral, or sad face for each question:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I follow game directions like 'Put the pencil in the bag'?" (Faces: Happy/Neutral/Sad)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I listen and say where things are, like 'On the table'?" (Faces: Happy/Neutral/Sad)</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Student and teacher reflection for future planning)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new classroom objects did you learn about today by listening?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy or difficult for you when listening to the teacher's instructions or your friend's questions?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What listening activities worked particularly well for these 3rd-grade students? Did they enjoy the "Classroom Scavenger Hunt"?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were the target prepositions (in, on, next to) clear to most students based on their actions and responses? What specific challenges did students face with listening comprehension (e.g., speed, specific vocabulary)?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolding might be needed for students struggling with listening skills in the next lesson? (e.g., more visual aids, slower speech, more repetition).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> "Today we listened and found objects in our classroom. Next time, in Lesson 2, we will *read* about these same objects and their locations in a story! We will see the words for 'book' and 'desk' and 'on' in writing."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>